According to the provided excerpt, preregistration is similar to a dissertation proposal where you lay out your research plan in advance [1]. This helps ensure that you don't change your methods after the study is completed [1]. You can also consider "registered reports," which is preregistration done with a specific journal with a publication agreement [1].



If you'd like to learn more, you can explore the following resources:

*   [Qualitative Open Science Practices](https://forrt.org/educators-corner/006-qualitative-os-practices/)
*   [Virtual Unconference on Open Scholarship Practices in Education Research](https://www.cos.io/education-reseach-2021-virtual-unconference#:~:text=February%208%2D9%2C%202021&text=The%20unconference%20included%20engaging%20plenary,detailed%20guidance%20for%20education%20researchers)
*   [Open Educational Resources website](https://www.oercommons.org/courseware/lesson/80058)

)

According to "A Student’s Guide to Open Science," the replication crisis has impacted research disciplines, including psychology [1]. The book addresses this crisis and the subsequent open research reform [1]. It aims to provide students (and educators) with a history of the replication crisis, its causes, and open science reform, including its benefits and challenges [1]. The book also offers practical guidance on implementing open science practices like preprints, preregistration, and open data [1].

FORRT and its members strive to overcome the gap in educational practices regarding the replication crisis [1]. FORRT provides several open educational resources, including a [bank of lesson plans](https://forrt.org/lesson-plans), [replications and reversals](https://forrt.org/reversals), and an [open science glossary](https://forrt.org/glossary) [1].

]
ReproCrowd is a platform designed to check numerical reproducibility within minutes [1]. You can use it to run a check for numerical reproducibility by following these steps: first, pick a paper with openly available data and code [1]. Then, use the [ReproCrowd app](https://forrt.org/reprocrowd/) to complete the process [1]. Finally, submit a standardized report to a GitHub repo, where it will be reviewed and signed [1].

The platform allows you to search by paper, field, or outcome and filter by verdict [1]. Each report links to the [FLoRA Replication Atlas](https://forrt.org/flora-replication-atlas) [1].
